# id: M68-restart-timestamp-derived-not-minted
# covering-test: TestRestartsMintTheirOwnStartTimestamp
# package: ./sim/hunt/
# expect: killed
# power-seeds: 60
# power-detector: sweep
# power-measured: sweep-detected at 60 seeds, two criteria: 52 start timestamps carrying another node's tag and 65 restarts that did not clear the commit they restarted on. It measured 0 of 1 and then 0 of 60 as a RATE, and both zeros were the instrument: this class's detector is an aggregate assertion and the probe consulted none of them until A6's close. ForeignTagStarts and StaleRestarts were also collected by every shard of the exit run and asserted nowhere, which is why the criteria had to be added before this could be measured at all (DESIGN-A6 sections 31, 35.1)
# mutates: BUG-021's SECOND half, and the reason the fix is one decision rather than two. A restarting transaction adopts RestartAt directly instead of minting above it. RestartAt is the observed commit's successor -- Logical plus one -- and Logical's low bits are the node tag, so the restarted transaction is identified by a number from another node's sequence. A tree carrying only the first half of the fix looks complete until a restart lands on a foreign tag
--- a/sim/hunt/bank.go
+++ b/sim/hunt/bank.go
@@ -439,7 +439,7 @@
 	// the half of option A that is easy to leave out, because a partial
 	// implementation looks exactly like a complete one until a restart lands on a
 	// foreign tag.
-	minted, node, ok := c.nowAboveFrom(t.ts, ts)
+	minted, node, ok := ts, t.ts%len(c.drivers), true
 	if !ok {
 		c.abandon(t)
 		return
